-A failed condition notifies at most **three times**, spaced by its check's
-cooldown, and then goes quiet until the set changes or clears. A new unit
-failing, or a disk that crosses into its next tier, is a change: that fires at
-once and starts the count again. Recovery clears the state, so the same failure
-later is news again (every check except `units` hands back its strikes and
-drops any mute but keeps its last-fired time, so one that flaps around its line
-still waits out its cooldown). This is why a dead `kded6` that has been failed since last
-week does not nag hourly.
-
-What counts as "the same condition" is deliberately coarse, because a changed
-message bypasses the cooldown. `disk` is keyed on a tier — warn from 85%,
-critical from 95% — not on the percentage: usage that wobbles 95↔96 is one
-card, going from warn to critical is a new one, and drifting back down is
-silent for the rest of that episode. `memory`, `downloads` and `updates` are
-keyed on a constant ("high", "big", "pending"); the card title carries the live
-number. Three points of slack (`disk` 82–84%, `memory` 89–91%) keep a reading
-that hovers at the line from counting as recovered and alerting again.
-
-A probe that fails is not a recovery. A `systemctl --failed` that times out, a
-`df` or `free` that prints nothing, a `du` or `git status` that hangs, a
-calendar that returns garbage or a `pgrep` that errors all end the pass with
-the state untouched, so a flaky bus cannot turn into a card per poll. `desktop`
-and `network` also look a second time a few seconds later before alarming, so a
-bar restarting for a theme change, or a route that drops while a VPN switches,
-stays quiet.
-
-**Mute** silences one exact condition — the exact unit set, the disk tier. A
-mute with no duration (or `forever`) holds until that condition changes; a mute
-with one (`1h`, `1d`) lapses by itself. A changed condition ignores the mute and
-alerts, because it is a different problem. A mute is dropped when its
-condition clears, and `--unmute` also gives a capped check its three strikes
-back. Durations are `90`, `15m`, `2h`, `1d`, `1w`; anything else is refused
-rather than read as "until it changes". `--list` shows which check is muted and
-until when.
-
-Every action shows a low "starting: …" notification the moment it is chosen,
-and runs detached, so a long cleanup never holds up a later check. Tools an
-action needs are looked up when the card is built (`command -v`) and fall back
-to something that needs nothing extra.
+Each check has a cooldown, so a disk that stays full notifies every six hours,
+not every five minutes. It re-notifies early only if the *value* changed.
 
