-**Do-not-disturb silences everything, including critical.** That is not what
-swaync does on its own: its notify path computes `bypass_dnd = urgency ==
-CRITICAL || hint SWAYNC_BYPASS_DND` and skips the DND check entirely when that
-is true, so a critical notification (which is everything `blackwall-watch`
-sends) pops while DND is on. An inhibitor does not help either — the same
-computed line short-circuits before inhibitors are looked at. The enforcement
-is a `muted` `notification-visibility` rule covering every urgency, applied
-when the notification is parsed, *before* that bypass is consulted: the popup
-is never drawn, and the notification still lands in the centre. `blackwall-quiet
-dnd` writes and removes the rule alongside the setting, and `blackwall-quiet
-dnd-watch` (started from `blackwall-autostart`) keeps it in step however DND
-was flipped, including from swaync's own panel switch.
-
-**A swaync crash or restart does not lose do-not-disturb.** swaync 0.12.6 has an
-upstream use-after-free that segfaults it now and then (a popup removed while
-the pointer is over it); systemd restarts it within about a second. Checked
-live with `systemctl --user restart swaync` and with `kill -KILL`: the daemon
-binds its DND property to gsettings `org.erikreider.swaync dnd-state` (dconf),
-reads it back on start and writes nothing on the way up, so DND stays on, and
-the `blackwall-dnd` rule is in `config.json`, which a restart does not touch.
-Critical notifications stay silent across the restart. What *is* lost is the
-notification-centre history (it is in memory only; `blackwall-notify`'s
-recorder log keeps the record). Do not hover popups to test this: that is the
-crash.
-
